│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ vectorize_balanced_packing                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The dominant cost of the current algorithm is `balanced_packing`: it runs a pure-Python   │
│                          │ double loop (num_layers × num_groups iterations) with a `min()` generator per item, plus  │
│                          │ a `.cpu()` sync and per-item Python indexing. Since the greedy least-loaded-pack          │
│                          │ assignment is sequential only within a single layer's item sequence, we can vectorize     │
│                          │ across all layers: process items in sorted order step-by-step, but for every step compute │
│                          │ the pack selection for ALL layers at once with tensor ops (masked argmin over pack        │
│                          │ weights, scatter_add updates). This reduces Python iterations from L×G to G, removing the │
│                          │ per-item Python overhead entirely and avoiding repeated syncs. The resulting assignment   │
│                          │ is identical to the original greedy (argmin with +inf masking on full packs breaks ties   │
│                          │ toward the lowest pack index, same as Python's `min`). Sorting stays on CPU but is done   │
│                          │ once with `argsort` on the whole tensor instead of per-layer `.cpu()` round-trips in a    │
│                          │ loop. This substantially cuts algorithm execution time (speed_score is currently 0.00,    │
│                          │ times_algorithm 0.55), while producing the same (or better) balancedness since the greedy │
│                          │ semantics are preserved.                                                                  │
