├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ vectorize_balanced_packing_snake                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The main bottleneck is `balanced_packing`: it runs a pure-Python double loop over every   │
│                          │ layer and every expert group, doing per-item greedy bin selection. This makes the         │
│                          │ algorithm's own runtime dominate (speed_score 0.00).                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Instead, replace the per-layer greedy with a fully vectorized "snake" (serpentine)        │
│                          │ packing: sort each layer's weights in descending order, cut the sorted list into          │
│                          │ `num_packs` consecutive chunks of `groups_per_pack` items, and assign chunk 0 to packs in │
│                          │ increasing order, chunk 1 in decreasing order, alternating. This is a classic,            │
│                          │ near-optimal heuristic for balanced partitioning of sorted weights: large items are       │
│                          │ spread first, and the alternating direction cancels the residual imbalance between        │
│                          │ consecutive chunks. It guarantees exactly `groups_per_pack` items per pack (same          │
│                          │ feasibility as before) while producing the same style of near-balanced sums.              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The whole computation becomes a handful of tensor ops (`sort`, `scatter_`) executed once  │
│                          │ for all layers simultaneously, eliminating both Python loops entirely. This should        │
│                          │ massively reduce execution time while keeping (and typically slightly improving) balance, │
│                          │ since greedy LPT-style per-item placement and snake assignment of sorted items are of     │
│                          │ comparable quality.                                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Note the return tensors now live on `weight.device` (same as before, since inputs are CPU │
│                          │ here), and rank_in_pack semantics (position within the pack) are preserved, so the        │
│                          │ hierarchical rebalancer downstream is unaffected.                                         │
