│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ vectorized_snake_packing                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The main bottleneck is `balanced_packing`: it sorts on GPU, moves to CPU, and then runs a │
│                          │ pure-Python greedy loop of O(num_layers × num_groups × num_packs) iterations with a       │
│                          │ `min(...)` generator each step — this dominates algorithm runtime (times_algorithm:       │
│                          │ 0.55).                                                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Replace the greedy assignment with a "snake" (boustrophedon) packing: sort items          │
│                          │ descending, then assign the j-th heaviest item to pack (j mod P) in even blocks and pack  │
│                          │ (P-1 - j mod P) in odd blocks, alternating direction each block. This guarantees exactly  │
│                          │ n/m items per pack (hard constraint preserved), places heavy items into different packs   │
│                          │ (same as greedy's first round), and alternates so that the 2nd heaviest round compensates │
│                          │ the 1st — empirically near-identical balancedness to greedy LPT while being fully         │
│                          │ vectorizable on GPU with no Python loops and no CPU transfer. This cuts algorithm time    │
│                          │ dramatically (improving times_algorithm and speed_score) with negligible balancedness     │
│                          │ loss.                                                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Additionally keep the computation entirely on the original device (no `.cpu()`            │
│                          │ roundtrip).                                                                               │
