│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ greedy_lpt_global_balancer                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of the hierarchical group→node→GPU packing pipeline (which constrains experts to  │
│                          │ stay inside pre-packed groups and uses slow per-item Python loops), this approach solves  │
│                          │ the problem globally in two fully-vectorized phases per layer: (1) replica allocation —   │
│                          │ the redundant expert slots are assigned one-by-one to the logical expert with the highest │
│                          │ per-replica load (weight/count), which minimizes the maximum per-replica load; (2) GPU    │
│                          │ packing — the expanded replicas are sorted in descending load and placed with LPT         │
│                          │ (Longest Processing Time first) greedy assignment onto the least-loaded GPU that still    │
│                          │ has capacity, which is the classical 4/3-approximation for balanced bin packing and       │
│                          │ directly minimizes the per-GPU load spread. Working entirely with numpy integer/float     │
│                          │ arrays (no per-item Python min() over packs, no torch sort round-trips to CPU inside      │
│                          │ loops) makes it dramatically faster, and removing the hierarchical group constraint       │
│                          │ improves global expert-level balancedness since hot experts can now be replicated across  │
│                          │ any GPUs.                                                                                 │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
