│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ global_replication_then_gpu_packing                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ The current algorithm replicates experts *within* each node (Step 2 runs                  │
│                          │ `replicate_experts` on node-local expert subsets with only `num_physical_experts //       │
│                          │ num_nodes` slots per node). This caps the achievable balance: a hot expert living on one  │
│                          │ node cannot receive replicas proportional to its global load. Following the               │
│                          │ recommendation, I swap the order: first replicate globally over the full                  │
│                          │ `num_physical_experts` pool, then (keeping the node-level group packing from Step 1 for   │
│                          │ network locality) adjust replica counts so each node holds exactly `num_physical_experts  │
│                          │ // num_nodes` replicas, and finally place replicas onto GPUs with an LPT-style greedy     │
│                          │ (min-load GPU with free slots) instead of the count-constrained `balanced_packing`. This  │
│                          │ removes the per-node replication granularity bottleneck, improves GPU balancedness, and   │
│                          │ replaces the O(n·m) Python `balanced_packing` loop for physical experts with a single     │
│                          │ greedy pass per layer/node (faster).                                                      │
