                        Patch Metadata - Gen 19/100 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ vectorize_packing_and_batch_replication                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ Three changes targeting both goals:                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Vectorize `balanced_packing`**: the current version has a pure-Python double loop    │
│                          │ (layer × groups) with a `min()` scan per item — this is why `times_algorithm` blew up to  │
│                          │ 0.55s. Replace with the fully vectorized greedy LPT from the earlier generation (loop     │
│                          │ only over item positions, all layer rows processed at once with `scatter_`/`argmin`),     │
│                          │ which scored `times_algorithm ≈ 0.01` with the same (or slightly better) balance quality  │
│                          │ (expert 0.25 vs 0.24).                                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Batch `replicate_experts` with one top-k**: the per-iteration scatter loop runs      │
│                          │ `num_redundant` kernel launches. Key insight: giving expert `e` its `j`-th duplicate has  │
│                          │ marginal gain `w/(j*(j+1))`, which strictly decreases in `j`. Therefore the greedy "pick  │
│                          │ max marginal gain each step" sequence is exactly the sorted top-k over the static         │
│                          │ candidate set `{(e, j)}` — a candidate `(e, j)` can never be selected before `(e, j-1)`   │
│                          │ since it has strictly smaller gain. So a single `topk` over `` candidates reproduces the  │
│                          │ greedy picks, replica ranks (`j`), and final counts in O(1) kernel launches instead of    │
│                          │ O(num_redundant).                                                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **Remove the `.cpu()` round-trip** in `rebalance_experts`: `weight.float().cpu()`      │
│                          │ forces a device copy and moves all subsequent ops (and the returned maps) off the GPU.    │
│                          │ Keeping the tensor on its original device lets all the sort/gather/scatter ops run        │
│                          │ on-device with float32, cutting the dominant inference-time cost noted in the             │
│                          │ recommendations.                                                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ These are mutually consistent: all three operate device-agnostically and on the same      │
│                          │ tensor layouts.                                                                           │
